diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically on its eth2 interface (the r6_r3 backbone link connecting to router3 and the rest of the network).

**Mechanism:** Router6 has four chained FORWARD rules all matching `iifname "eth2"`: one narrow ACCEPT for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, followed by DROP rules for TCP, UDP, and a catch-all DROP for all other protocols (including ICMP). This means every packet arriving at router6 from the backbone direction is silently discarded — whether it is traffic destined for 172.18.51.x hosts (forward direction) or ICMP TTL-Exceeded replies from upstream routers destined back to those same hosts (return direction). Live counters confirm 25 UDP and 55 ICMP/other packets have already been dropped. Router6 self-originated pings to 172.18.51.18 work because self-generated traffic uses the OUTPUT chain, not FORWARD.

**Localization:** Router6, eth2 (r6_r3 link, 10.50.1.2/24), iptables FORWARD chain. The 172.18.51.0/24 subnet behind mswitch2/switch10 is completely isolated from all external subnets (172.16.10.x, 172.16.20.x, 192.168.200.x, and others). The user-reported inter-VLAN fault (laptop0 VLAN 20 → pc4 VLAN 10) is a false alarm — that path transits router0 exclusively and works correctly.

**Root cause:** An ACL injected into router6's iptables FORWARD chain blocks all traffic ingressing on the backbone interface eth2, effectively black-holing the entire 172.18.51.0/24 segment.

**Fix:** Remove the four FORWARD chain rules on router6 (`iptables -F FORWARD` and equivalent nftables entries), or replace them with a policy that permits legitimate inter-subnet traffic from the backbone while preserving any intended access controls.
=== END ===